NAME
       herd - package manager for Cervus

SYNOPSIS
       herd [--progress=STYLE] update
       herd search TERM
       herd list
       herd available
       herd info NAME
       herd install NAME...
       herd upgrade [NAME...]
       herd remove NAME...
       herd boot-status
       herd update-kernel
       herd update-bootloader [limine|grub]

DESCRIPTION
       herd installs pre-built packages from a remote repository. A
       package is a gzipped ustar archive rooted at /, described by a
       manifest in the repository index.

       The repository URL comes from $HERD_REPO or the repo= line in
       /etc/herd.conf. On 'update' the index is downloaded together with
       its Ed25519 signature and checked against the public key in
       /etc/herd.pub; an index that does not verify is refused. Because
       the signed index carries each package's sha256, 'install' needs
       only match the downloaded tarball against the index to trust it.

       A command that needs the index fetches it first if there is not
       one yet, so a fresh install does not have to 'update' by hand.

       Anything that writes outside your home directory asks for your
       password, unless you are already root.

OPTIONS
       -y, --yes
              Agree to the dependencies and the upgrade list without
              asking. Without a terminal herd will not guess, so scripts
              need this.

       --progress=STYLE
              How to draw download progress: bar (the default), pacman,
              hash, dots, percent, or none. It can also be set with
              $HERD_PROGRESS or a progress= line in /etc/herd.conf.
              Progress is drawn only when stderr is a terminal.

COMMANDS
       update        download and verify the package index
       search TERM   list packages whose name or summary contains TERM
       list          list installed packages and their versions
       available     list every package the repository offers, marking
                     the ones already installed
       info NAME     print the index entry for NAME
       install NAME  install NAME and, before it, anything it depends on.
                     Anything pulled in that you did not name is listed
                     first and installed only if you agree.
       upgrade       replace everything installed with the newer version
                     in the index, or just the packages you name. The
                     base system (cervus-base, cervus-libc, cervus-media
                     and kernel) is registered like any other package, so
                     a plain 'herd upgrade' updates the whole machine.
       remove NAME   delete the files a package installed

       boot-status   find the boot partition and say which bootloader
                     is on it and where the kernel lives
       update-kernel install the 'kernel' package onto the boot
                     partition, keeping the running kernel as
                     /boot/kernel.old so a bad one can be backed out
       update-bootloader [NAME]
                     reinstall the bootloader already in use, or, with
                     a name, install a different one. Installing a
                     second bootloader is not an upgrade - both claim
                     the same disk and a mistake stops the machine
                     booting - so it asks before doing it.

FILES
       /etc/herd.conf         repo= and optional progress= lines
       /etc/herd.pub          Ed25519 public key, as hex
       /var/lib/herd/INDEX    the last downloaded index
       /var/lib/herd/*.files  the file list of each installed package
       /var/lib/herd/reboot-required
                              written when an update replaced the kernel
                              or part of the base system; the shell says
                              so at login and init clears it on boot

SEE ALSO
       tar(1), wget(1)
